## Context

Two holes, both found by measuring the corpus rather than by reading it, and
both left open by every document currently in the recipe.

**Hole 1 — bulk and a thin text layer never meet.** The records with a thin or
absent text layer stop at 528 pages (`ibn-khaldun-1904-muqaddimah`); every record
above 300 pages with real bulk carries dense text throughout. Nothing in the
corpus has text concentrated in a minority of its pages.

**Hole 2 — page count.** The corpus tops out at 1 104 pages
(`johnson-1785-dictionary`). The document that stalled the sitter on the author's
own library (ticket 0793) is 3 949 pages. Nothing here is within a factor of
three of it.

## The two documents

**`gri_33125001118088`** — *Recueil de planches*, Getty Research Institute scan,
1762. 758 pages, 42 MB, Internet Archive, public domain by age.

Measured per page, not averaged — the average is what made this document look
uninteresting at first, and it is worth recording why. Characters per page:

| chars/page | pages | share |
|---|---|---|
| 0 – 50 | 464 | 61 % |
| 50 – 200 | 156 | 21 % |
| 200 – 5 000 | 23 | 3 % |
| 5 000 + | 115 | 15 % |

Median 25, mean 1 199 — and only 2 pages of 758 fall in the band the mean names.
The two modes are 31 and 6 639 characters. A text volume of the same
Encyclopédie, measured the same way as control, is unimodal: 97 % of its pages
between 3 000 and 6 461, median 4 916.

The structure is blocky rather than alternating: each subject's explication runs
as consecutive dense pages, then its plates run near-empty. Pages 9-20 hold
6 076, 6 289, 5 529, 5 888…; pages 380-403 hold 0, 20, 0, 151, 0, 141, 0, 96…

The two caps land in unrelated places, which is the point:

| | plates volume | control text volume |
|---|---|---|
| 500 000th character | page 258 of 758 | page 112 of 1 040 |
| thin pages before the 100-page cap | 77 % | 7 % |

Locators for `cap_expectations`, already read off: page 9 (6 076 chars, dense,
before the page cap), page 101 (0 chars, thin, after), page 109 (7 050 chars,
dense, after). A zero-character plate page immediately after a 7 050-character
explication page, on both sides of the cap, is a case no current document can
produce.

Proposed mechanism name: `text-concentrated-in-minority-of-pages`. Not
`sparse-captions` — the sparseness is a property of the distribution, not of the
pages.

**`desertquartzites00unse_1`** — Desert Quartzite Solar Project, *draft* plan
amendment and environmental impact statement, US Department of the Interior,
Bureau of Land Management. 3 666 pages, 224 MB, Internet Archive.

Licence basis is settled precedent, not a new category: the recipe already holds
`epa-2016-ghg-inventory-1990-2014` ("US federal government work (EPA), public
domain in the United States under 17 U.S.C. §105") and
`doe-2011-quadrennial-technology-review`.

## Three decisions taken in session, with their reasons

**Malynes stays.** Removing it was considered as a way to pay for the new bytes.
It cannot be done. `absent-from-library` appears exactly once across the 86
`expected_miss` questions, on q-0149, whose stated purpose is to separate "the
library answered badly" from "the library has no such record at all". Malynes is
the corpus's only control for that distinction. `oversized-source-file` would go
with it too. And the saving is illusory: Malynes was never ingested — its 352 MB
source is above the sourcing run's 300 MB cap, the export holds 103 parents for
104 recipe records, and its bytes live only in the git-ignored fetch cache.

**The final EIS is rejected in favour of the draft.** `desertquartzites00unse_0`
is 4 548 pages but 489 MB, above the same 300 MB sourcing cap. It would not
become the corpus's large report; it would become a second Malynes — declared,
never ingested, useful only as another absence control. One is enough. The draft
at 224 MB clears the cap and sits within 7 % of the AR6 on both pages and bytes.

**The draft/final pair is rejected.** The relation it would demonstrate,
`near-duplicate-publication`, is already declared twice on the Bettencourt
preprint/published pair, at a few MB. Adding 713 MB — 57 % of the current 1.25 GB
cache — for a relation already covered does not hold. A near-duplicate at
four-thousand-page scale is a *retrieval* property and belongs to the scoring
lane, not here; if that lane ever needs it, the pair is on the Archive.

## Relevant files

- `bench/fixtures/recipe.json` — where both records go, at the ruled shape of 0721
- `bench/fixtures/archives.json` — internet-archive is already admitted
- `bench/fixtures/fetch_recipe.py` — refuses a malformed record before fetching
- `bench/fixtures/README.md` — the census table and licence bases to update
- `bench/fixtures/questions/` — where the new questions land
- `tests/test_fixture_recipe.py` — the refusals that must keep firing

## Actions

1. Add both records to `recipe.json` at the 0721 shape: topic, stratum,
   per-attachment language and charset, relations labelled truthfully,
   `license_basis`, `provenance_check`, `page_count`.
2. Pin the bytes: sha256 of each, `bytes_url`, `archive`, `identifier`.
3. Fill `cap_expectations` for the plates volume from the locators above, and
   measure the equivalent locators for the EIS the same way.
4. Read the EIS title page for its publication year — the Archive record has no
   `year` field, and the ruled shape wants one.
5. Register `text-concentrated-in-minority-of-pages` as a mechanism.
6. Write the questions: at least one whose answer sits on a dense explication
   page past the 100-page cap, and one whose answer sits on a plate page whose
   caption is the only text.
7. Update the README census table and the shape paragraph.

## Test

Method for step 3, so it need not be rediscovered: the Archive's
`_hocr_pageindex.json.gz` derivative holds one entry per page,
`[searchtext_start, searchtext_end, hocr_start, hocr_end]`, so a page's character
count is the difference of the first two. It weighs about 10 KB.

**Check the unit before trusting any number from it: `len(index)` must equal the
item's `imagecount`.** Two derivatives were tried first and both gave a plausible
wrong answer — `_djvu.txt` is a flat concatenation with no page separator and
yielded a single page of 939 329 characters; a `_hocr_searchtext` line is a text
block, not a page, and reported 16 082 pages for a 1 040-page volume. Neither
failure announced itself.

The red step is the control that caught them: run the measurement on a document
whose shape is known — a text volume of the same Encyclopédie — and require it to
come back unimodal. A distribution probe that cannot distinguish a unimodal
document from a bimodal one has not been shown to work.

## Verification

- [ ] `len(pageindex) == imagecount` asserted for every item measured
- [ ] Control volume comes back unimodal; plates volume bimodal
- [ ] `fetch_recipe.py` accepts both records and the sha256 of both matches
- [ ] Both files under the 300 MB sourcing cap, so both actually reach the export
- [ ] README census figures recomputed, not edited by hand

## Invariants

- `malynes-1622-lex-mercatoria` and q-0149 are untouched.
- No new archive admitted; no new licence basis invented.
- Existing question ids keep their subjects.
- `make check` passes.

## Exit criteria

- Both records in `recipe.json`, bytes pinned, fetched and hash-verified.
- The plates volume's `cap_expectations` carry real page locators, not prose.
- At least two new questions, each naming the page its answer sits on.
- README census updated and consistent with the recipe.
